1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,789
Welcome to lectures twenty one and twenty two:

2
00:00:03,789 --> 00:00:06,424
encoding and imbalanced classes.

3
00:00:06,424 --> 00:00:10,872
Turning words into numbers, and handling rare classes.

4
00:00:11,672 --> 00:00:27,372
The lecture covers why we encode, types of categories, label and ordinal encoding, one-hot encoding, dummy variables, many categories, target encoding as a concept, and imbalanced classes.

5
00:00:27,372 --> 00:00:34,136
The practical runs each idea on real Airbnb data and repeats on a second dataset.

6
00:00:34,936 --> 00:00:37,357
A model can only read numbers.

7
00:00:37,357 --> 00:00:44,295
A model multiplies values by weights, and it cannot multiply Brooklyn or Private room.

8
00:00:44,295 --> 00:00:53,574
In our Airbnb data, room type has three categories, borough has five, and neighbourhood has two hundred twenty one.

9
00:00:53,574 --> 00:00:58,335
The fix is to change every word column into number columns.

10
00:00:58,335 --> 00:01:04,144
This is called encoding, and how we do it changes what the model learns.

11
00:01:04,944 --> 00:01:05,833
First ask:

12
00:01:05,833 --> 00:01:08,680
does the category have an order?

13
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:13,838
Nominal categories have no order, like borough or colours.

14
00:01:13,838 --> 00:01:22,376
Ordinal categories have an order, like shared room, private room, entire home, or T-shirt sizes.

15
00:01:22,376 --> 00:01:27,980
Binary categories have only two values, like weekend yes or no.

16
00:01:27,980 --> 00:01:30,648
The answer picks the encoding.

17
00:01:31,448 --> 00:01:35,170
Label encoding gives one number per category:

18
00:01:35,170 --> 00:01:41,291
Bronx zero, Brooklyn one, Manhattan two, Queens three, Staten Island four.

19
00:01:41,291 --> 00:01:45,510
The problem is that the numbers look like an order.

20
00:01:45,510 --> 00:01:53,450
The model thinks Staten Island, four, is bigger than Manhattan, two, but boroughs have no order.

21
00:01:53,450 --> 00:01:59,240
Use it only for tree models and for the target column of a classifier.

22
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:03,128
For nominal columns in linear models, avoid it.

23
00:02:03,928 --> 00:02:08,144
With ordinal encoding you write the order yourself.

24
00:02:08,144 --> 00:02:16,823
Shared room is zero, private room is one, entire home is two, and the median price goes up with the code:

25
00:02:16,823 --> 00:02:20,046
forty five, seventy, one sixty dollars.

26
00:02:20,046 --> 00:02:21,865
Why write it yourself?

27
00:02:21,865 --> 00:02:28,312
Alphabetical order would give entire zero and shared two, the wrong way round.

28
00:02:28,312 --> 00:02:31,288
When there is a real order, keep it.

29
00:02:32,088 --> 00:02:36,457
One-hot encoding makes one zero-one column per category.

30
00:02:36,457 --> 00:02:41,528
Each home gets a one in its borough's column and zeros elsewhere.

31
00:02:41,528 --> 00:02:50,735
There is no false order, and each row has exactly one one, like an attendance sheet with one tick in the right column.

32
00:02:50,735 --> 00:02:55,104
One-hot is the safe choice for categories with no order.

33
00:02:55,904 --> 00:02:59,719
With dummy variables, keep k minus one columns.

34
00:02:59,719 --> 00:03:10,109
If a home is not in Brooklyn, Manhattan, Queens or Staten Island, it must be in the Bronx, so the Bronx column adds nothing new.

35
00:03:10,109 --> 00:03:15,710
Five boroughs means keeping four columns, with drop first equal true.

36
00:03:15,710 --> 00:03:22,122
The dropped one, the Bronx, is the baseline, and each dummy is read against it:

37
00:03:22,122 --> 00:03:27,155
Manhattan costs about eighty five dollars more than the Bronx.

38
00:03:27,155 --> 00:03:31,376
Keeping all five columns is the dummy variable trap.

39
00:03:32,176 --> 00:03:34,713
What about too many categories?

40
00:03:34,713 --> 00:03:42,734
Neighbourhood has two hundred twenty one, which would mean two hundred twenty one one-hot columns.

41
00:03:42,734 --> 00:03:47,644
Frequency encoding replaces each area by its share of homes:

42
00:03:47,644 --> 00:03:53,209
Williamsburg is zero point oh eight, Harlem zero point oh five four.

43
00:03:53,209 --> 00:03:54,764
That is one column.

44
00:03:54,764 --> 00:04:00,084
And a multi-class target like room type just needs class numbers:

45
00:04:00,084 --> 00:04:03,112
entire zero, private one, shared two.

46
00:04:03,912 --> 00:04:09,328
Target encoding replaces a category by its average target value.

47
00:04:09,328 --> 00:04:15,760
Williamsburg becomes its median price of one oh four, Tribeca three hundred.

48
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:16,607
Two rules.

49
00:04:16,607 --> 00:04:17,368
Rule one:

50
00:04:17,368 --> 00:04:22,277
use training rows only, or the model peeks at the answers.

51
00:04:22,277 --> 00:04:23,039
Rule two:

52
00:04:23,039 --> 00:04:25,155
do not trust tiny groups.

53
00:04:25,155 --> 00:04:37,003
One eight hundred dollar home in Fort Wadsworth cannot speak for the whole area, so groups with fewer than ten homes get the overall median.

54
00:04:37,003 --> 00:04:44,112
Two hundred twenty one columns become one useful column, if we follow the two rules.

55
00:04:44,912 --> 00:04:48,787
When one class is rare, accuracy can fool you.

56
00:04:48,787 --> 00:04:53,336
Only two point four percent of homes are shared rooms.

57
00:04:53,336 --> 00:05:01,844
A lazy model that always says entire home is fifty two percent correct but never finds a shared room.

58
00:05:01,844 --> 00:05:05,888
Check the class sizes before you trust accuracy.

59
00:05:06,688 --> 00:05:08,357
Four simple fixes.

60
00:05:08,357 --> 00:05:09,192
Stratify:

61
00:05:09,192 --> 00:05:14,477
keep the same percentage of each class in train and test.

62
00:05:14,477 --> 00:05:15,775
Class weights:

63
00:05:15,775 --> 00:05:19,763
make a mistake on the rare class cost more.

64
00:05:19,763 --> 00:05:20,597
Resample:

65
00:05:20,597 --> 00:05:26,624
copy rare rows or remove some common rows, on training rows only.

66
00:05:26,624 --> 00:05:28,850
And use the right score:

67
00:05:28,850 --> 00:05:33,208
check recall for each class, not just accuracy.

68
00:05:34,008 --> 00:05:35,762
Which encoding when?

69
00:05:35,762 --> 00:05:38,218
No order and few categories:

70
00:05:38,218 --> 00:05:42,953
one-hot with drop first, borough becomes four columns.

71
00:05:42,953 --> 00:05:44,093
Has an order:

72
00:05:44,093 --> 00:05:47,777
your own map, room type to zero, one, two.

73
00:05:47,777 --> 00:05:49,180
Many categories:

74
00:05:49,180 --> 00:05:55,933
frequency or target encoding, two hundred twenty one areas become one column.

75
00:05:55,933 --> 00:05:58,914
A target with two or more classes:

76
00:05:58,914 --> 00:06:00,230
label encoding.

77
00:06:00,230 --> 00:06:02,598
A rare class in the target:

78
00:06:02,598 --> 00:06:05,667
stratify, class weights and recall.

79
00:06:05,667 --> 00:06:11,280
Look at the column type and the number of categories, then pick.

